{"id": "mitre-m02", "question": "Besides keeping command and control working when the main channel is blocked, what other reason does ATT&CK give for using fallback channels?", "reference": "To avoid data transfer thresholds.", "relevant": [{"path": "techniques/T1008_fallback-channels.md", "evidence": "to avoid data transfer thresholds"}], "answerable": true, "tags": ["base", "has-variants", "medium", "technique", "enterprise"], "notes": "The answer is half a sentence, and the file has 66 bullets of procedure noise that retrieval has to rank past. Context (not required in an answer): T1008 Fallback Channels."}
{"id": "mitre-m03", "question": "Which group used OnionDuke, in which years, and what did it fall back to for command and control?", "reference": "APT29, from 2013 to 2015. OnionDuke used Twitter as a backup C2 channel.", "relevant": [{"path": "software/S0052_onionduke.md", "evidence": "OnionDuke uses Twitter as a backup C2"}], "answerable": true, "tags": ["base", "has-variants", "medium", "software", "enterprise"], "notes": "Three facts from one short file. MiniDuke and CozyCar (also APT29, also Twitter) are decoys."}
{"id": "mitre-m04", "question": "What is the difference between ATT&CK's System Firmware and Component Firmware sub-techniques of Pre-OS Boot?", "reference": "System Firmware means modifying the BIOS or (U)EFI, the firmware between the OS and the hardware; Component Firmware means modifying the firmware of other components or devices, such as hard drives, so code runs outside the OS and the main system firmware.", "relevant": [{"path": "techniques/T1542.001_pre-os-boot-system-firmware.md", "evidence": "modify system firmware to persist on systems"}, {"path": "techniques/T1542.002_pre-os-boot-component-firmware.md", "evidence": "modify component firmware to persist on systems"}], "answerable": true, "tags": ["base", "has-variants", "medium", "technique", "comparison", "enterprise"], "notes": "Needs both files. Top-1 search can only return one, so answer correctness matters more than hit@1 here. Context (not required in an answer): T1542.001 vs T1542.002; those components may have weaker integrity checking."}
{"id": "mitre-m05", "question": "Why was Operation Honeybee given that name, and who did it target?", "reference": "It was named after the author name in its malicious Word documents, and it targeted humanitarian aid and inter-Korean affairs organizations.", "relevant": [{"path": "campaigns/C0006_operation-honeybee.md", "evidence": "named the campaign Honeybee after the author name"}], "answerable": true, "tags": ["base", "has-variants", "medium", "campaign", "enterprise"], "notes": "Both facts are in the description. The question names the campaign, so this tests reading more than retrieval. Context (not required in an answer): First in South Korea, later also Vietnam, Singapore, Japan, Indonesia, Argentina and Canada."}
{"id": "mitre-m06", "question": "What other name is PROMETHIUM known by, and which country's targets does it focus on?", "reference": "StrongPity; it focuses heavily on targets in Turkey.", "relevant": [{"path": "groups/G0056_promethium.md", "evidence": "heavy emphasis on Turkish targets"}], "answerable": true, "tags": ["base", "has-variants", "medium", "group", "enterprise", "mobile"], "notes": "StrongPity is also the name of PROMETHIUM's malware (S0491), which is the decoy. NEODYMIUM is mentioned as a similar group. Context (not required in an answer): Active since at least 2012."}
