{"id": "mitre-vh03", "question": "Operation Honeybee and the StrongPity group both signed their malware to look legitimate. Where did each get its signing certificate?", "reference": "Operation Honeybee stole an Adobe Systems digital signature; PROMETHIUM (StrongPity) created its own self-signed certificates.", "relevant": [{"path": "techniques/T1553.002_subvert-trust-controls-code-signing.md", "evidence": "stolen Adobe Systems digital signature", "group": "honeybee"}, {"path": "campaigns/C0006_operation-honeybee.md", "evidence": "stolen Adobe Systems digital signature", "group": "honeybee"}, {"path": "groups/G0056_promethium.md", "evidence": "created self-signed certificates to sign malicious installers"}], "answerable": true, "tags": ["base", "has-variants", "very-hard", "campaign", "group", "alias", "comparison", "enterprise"], "notes": "'StrongPity group' is an alias for PROMETHIUM and also the name of its malware. T1553.002 is the only file holding both answers, among dozens of other code-signing procedures. Context (not required in an answer): Honeybee used the stolen signature on its MaoCheng dropper; PROMETHIUM signed its malicious installers. Gold: the two Honeybee entries are alternatives (group 'honeybee'); the PROMETHIUM entry is required separately."}
{"id": "mitre-vh04", "question": "Which threat group ran the Korean-language implant that leaked stolen data through a South Korean webmail service around the 2018 Winter Games?", "reference": "Kimsuky (G0094).", "relevant": [{"path": "software/S0252_brave-prince.md", "evidence": "2018 Pyeongchang Winter Olympics"}], "answerable": true, "tags": ["base", "has-variants", "very-hard", "group", "multi-hop", "enterprise"], "notes": "No names given. Resolving 'webmail' to Daum, 'Winter Games' to Pyeongchang and the implant to Brave Prince leads to Kimsuky. Decoys: Gold Dragon and RunningRAT (same operations) and TRANSLATEXT (also mentions Daum). Context (not required in an answer): The implant is Brave Prince (S0252), seen alongside Gold Dragon around the 2018 Pyeongchang Winter Olympics; some variants exfiltrated through Daum's email service."}
{"id": "mitre-vh05", "question": "In its first documented mobile campaign, how did the group also tracked as StrongPity get its Android spyware onto victims' phones, and what kinds of data did the spyware take?", "reference": "Through a trojanized version of a legitimate app distributed via the compromised official Syrian E-Gov website. The spyware collected call logs, contacts, SMS messages, notifications from 17 apps, call recordings, location, Wi-Fi details, installed apps and file lists.", "relevant": [{"path": "campaigns/C0033_c0033.md", "evidence": "compromised official Syrian E-Gov website"}], "answerable": true, "tags": ["base", "has-variants", "very-hard", "campaign", "alias", "multi-hop", "mobile"], "notes": "Three steps: resolve the StrongPity alias to PROMETHIUM, find its first mobile campaign (C0033, which has an uninformative name), then gather about ten data types across many procedure lines. Judge partial lists generously. Context (not required in an answer): Campaign C0033."}
