{"id": "mitre-m10", "question": "Why can't an ordinary Android app list running processes on Android 7 and later, and how did apps get that list on older versions?", "reference": "The Android kernel uses the hidepid mount feature; before Android 7, apps could use the ps command or read the /proc directory.", "relevant": [{"path": "techniques/T1424_process-discovery.md", "evidence": "the Android kernel utilizing the hidepid mount feature. Prior to Android 7, applications could utilize the ps command or examine the /proc directory"}], "answerable": true, "tags": ["medium", "technique", "mobile", "base", "temporal"], "notes": "From T1424 Process Discovery's description. Verified: 'hidepid' appears only in T1424; M1006 only says Android 7 'introduced changes' without naming the mechanism. The iOS sysctl detail is kept out of the reference."}
{"id": "mitre-m11", "question": "When did the Operation Dust Storm actors start using Android backdoors, and where were the victims of that mobile activity located?", "reference": "By 2015; all identified victims at the time were in Japan or South Korea.", "relevant": [{"path": "campaigns/C0016_operation-dust-storm.md", "evidence": "also began to use Android backdoors in their operations by 2015, with all identified victims at the time residing in Japan or South Korea"}], "answerable": true, "tags": ["medium", "campaign", "mobile", "enterprise", "base", "dates"], "notes": "C0016 is in both Enterprise and Mobile; this is the only mobile-specific sentence of its description. Verified: the 'During Operation Dust Storm ... Android backdoors' procedure lines in T1420/T1533/T1636.004/T1646 give neither the year nor the countries, and 'Japan or South Korea' occurs only in C0016."}
{"id": "mitre-m12", "question": "Which other botnet cluster overlaps with the KV Botnet that Volt Typhoon relied on, and who took that botnet down?", "reference": "The JDY cluster; US law enforcement disrupted it.", "relevant": [{"path": "campaigns/C0035_kv-botnet-activity.md", "evidence": "it overlaps with another botnet cluster referred to as the JDY cluster. This botnet was disrupted by US law enforcement entities in early 2024"}], "answerable": true, "tags": ["medium", "campaign", "enterprise", "base", "iot", "real-incident"], "notes": "Two-part. Verified: 'JDY' and the takedown appear only in C0035; G1017 Volt Typhoon and the technique files mention KV Botnet but not the overlap or the disruption. Timing (early 2024, after activity from Oct 2022) kept out of the reference."}
{"id": "mitre-m13", "question": "The malware that wiped modems and routers during the 2022 ViaSat KA-SAT outage was built for which processor architecture?", "reference": "MIPS.", "relevant": [{"path": "software/S1125_acidrain.md", "evidence": "AcidRain is an ELF binary targeting modems and routers using MIPS architecture"}], "answerable": true, "tags": ["medium", "software", "enterprise", "base", "iot", "real-incident"], "notes": "The outage has to be resolved to AcidRain (S1125); G0034 Sandworm Team also mentions KA-SAT but not the architecture. Verified: 'MIPS' occurs only in S1125. Its variant AcidPour (S1167) targets x86, a tempting wrong answer."}
{"id": "mitre-m14", "question": "Which group is Fuxnet tied to, and whose infrastructure was it built to hit?", "reference": "The Blackjack hacking group; the industrial network infrastructure that manages control-system sensors for utility operations in Moscow.", "relevant": [{"path": "software/S1157_fuxnet.md", "evidence": "managing control system sensors for utility operations in Moscow. Fuxnet is linked to an entity referred to as the Blackjack hacking group"}], "answerable": true, "tags": ["medium", "software", "ics", "base", "attribution"], "notes": "Two-part. Verified: 'Blackjack' and the Moscow targeting appear only in S1157; Fuxnet procedure lines in T0806/T0809/T0814 etc. describe its actions, not its sponsor or target. Blackjack's assessed link to Ukrainian intelligence services kept out of the reference."}
{"id": "mitre-m15", "question": "We're setting up role-based access for protection relays and other IEDs at our substations. Which standards does ATT&CK's ICS mitigation on enforcing authorization point to, and what does each one cover?", "reference": "IEC 62351, which gives example roles for common system operations in the electric power sector, and IEEE 1686, which defines standard permissions for IED users.", "relevant": [{"path": "mitigations/M0800_authorization-enforcement.md", "evidence": "IEC 62351 provides examples of roles used to support common system operations within the electric power sector, while IEEE 1686 defines standard permissions"}], "answerable": true, "tags": ["medium", "mitigation", "ics", "base", "list-answer"], "notes": "Gold is M0800 Authorization Enforcement. Verified: both standards appear only in M0800's description; its 'Techniques addressed' lines (and the technique files' mitigation lines) give RBAC advice without naming any standard."}
{"id": "mitre-m16", "question": "Did the December 2025 wiper attacks on Polish wind and solar farms and a CHP plant take down electricity generation or heat supply?", "reference": "No. Neither electricity generation nor heat supply was impacted.", "relevant": [{"path": "campaigns/C0063_2025-poland-wiper-attacks.md", "evidence": "disrupted communications between affected facilities and the distribution system operator, but did not impact electricity generation or heat supply"}], "answerable": true, "tags": ["medium", "campaign", "ics", "enterprise", "base", "yes-no", "impact", "real-incident", "recent"], "notes": "Gold is C0063 2025 Poland Wiper Attacks. Verified: the no-impact statement occurs only in C0063; the T0827/T0829 procedure lines mention lost communications with the DSO but say nothing about generation. The communications disruption is kept out of the reference."}
{"id": "mitre-m17", "question": "Has the AI-framework flaw behind the ShadowRay attacks been patched, and if not, why not?", "reference": "No. It remains unpatched because the vendor disputes it, saying Ray is not meant to be used outside a strictly controlled network environment.", "relevant": [{"path": "campaigns/C0045_shadowray.md", "evidence": "remains unpatched and has been disputed by the vendor as they maintain that Ray is not intended for use outside of a strictly controlled network"}], "answerable": true, "tags": ["medium", "campaign", "enterprise", "base", "yes-no", "real-incident"], "notes": "Two-part (patched? why?). The flaw is CVE-2023-48022 in Ray; T1190 also names that CVE for ShadowRay but says nothing about it being unpatched or disputed. Verified: 'remains unpatched', 'disputed by the vendor' and 'strictly controlled network' occur only in C0045."}
{"id": "mitre-m18", "question": "We run Kubernetes. To catch container breakouts, which pod settings does ATT&CK's detection strategy tell us to look for in the API server logs?", "reference": "Pod specs that use a hostPath volume or a privileged securityContext.", "relevant": [{"path": "techniques/T1611_escape-to-host.md", "evidence": "docker:daemon (container create/start with privileged flag or host volume mount); kubernetes:apiserver (Pod spec with hostPath or privileged securityContext)"}], "answerable": true, "tags": ["medium", "detection", "enterprise", "base"], "notes": "The system has to map 'container breakout' to T1611 Escape to Host and read analytic AN0612 of DET0219, which sits in the middle of the file. Verified: 'securityContext' and 'Pod spec with hostPath' occur only in T1611; other kubernetes:apiserver log sources in the corpus cover different behaviours."}
{"id": "mitre-h11", "question": "An iOS app apparently made it past Apple's store vetting, and analysts found nothing actually malicious in it, yet it is still treated as a security risk. Which software is that in ATT&CK?", "reference": "ZergHelper (S0287).", "relevant": [{"path": "software/S0287_zerghelper.md", "evidence": "ZergHelper is iOS riskware that was unique due to its apparent evasion of Apple's App Store review process"}], "answerable": true, "tags": ["hard", "software", "mobile", "base", "id-lookup"], "notes": "Indirect description: the system has to identify the object from its description. Verified: 'App Store review' occurs only in S0287; ZergHelper's procedure lines in T1407/T1476 mention code updating and certificate abuse, not the review evasion."}
{"id": "mitre-h12", "question": "Where are the operators of the Android RAT HilalRAT from, and whom have they targeted?", "reference": "Iran; they have targeted people in the Middle East.", "relevant": [{"path": "groups/G1029_unc788.md", "evidence": "UNC788 is a group of hackers from Iran that has targeted people in the Middle East"}], "answerable": true, "tags": ["hard", "group", "mobile", "base", "attribution"], "notes": "Needs the hop HilalRAT -> UNC788 (G1029); the group file lists HilalRAT under its software, so it alone answers. Verified: the HilalRAT file and T1660 name UNC788 but never say Iran or the Middle East; 'hackers from Iran' occurs only in G1029."}
{"id": "mitre-h13", "question": "A user scanned a QR code dressed up as a WhatsApp group invite, which linked their account to an attacker's device. What does WhatsApp then do on its own that gives the attacker the user's past conversations?", "reference": "The user's device automatically sends an end-to-end encrypted copy of recent message history to the adversary-controlled device.", "relevant": [{"path": "techniques/T1676_linked-devices.md", "evidence": "Upon scanning the QR code in WhatsApp, the user's device will automatically send an end-to-end encrypted copy of recent message history"}], "answerable": true, "tags": ["hard", "technique", "mobile", "base", "recent"], "notes": "Scenario for T1676 Linked Devices (a recent technique, last modified 2025-05); the system must map it to the technique and pick the WhatsApp detail rather than Signal's manual 'Transfer Message History'. Verified: Star Blizzard/Sandworm files mention the linked-devices feature but not the automatic history copy; 'recent message history' occurs only in T1676."}
{"id": "mitre-h14", "question": "Some of our iPhones were hit by a zero-click iMessage exploit chain that ended with the TriangleDB implant. Which threat group does ATT&CK attribute that campaign to?", "reference": "None: ATT&CK says Operation Triangulation (C0054) was carried out by unidentified actors.", "relevant": [{"path": "campaigns/C0054_operation-triangulation.md", "evidence": "The unidentified actors used zero-click exploits in iMessage attachments to gain Initial Access"}], "answerable": true, "tags": ["hard", "campaign", "mobile", "base", "attribution", "negative-answer"], "notes": "The system must identify C0054 from the scenario and report that it has no attributed group (a model may be tempted to name a vendor or nation state from outside knowledge). Verified: only C0054 describes its actors as unidentified; the TriangleDB and Binary Validator files list only the campaign under 'Used by'."}
{"id": "mitre-h15", "question": "On a gas station's fuel management controller we found an ARM Linux backdoor; the same family has been reported on IP cameras, routers and PLCs in Israel and the US. Which ATT&CK software entry is it, and which group has used it?", "reference": "IOControl (S9046), used by CyberAv3ngers.", "relevant": [{"path": "software/S9046_iocontrol.md", "evidence": "IOControl has been used by CyberAv3ngers which is suspected to be affiliated with the Iranian Government"}], "answerable": true, "tags": ["hard", "software", "ics", "enterprise", "base", "iot", "attribution"], "notes": "Indirect: the malware is never named; fuel management systems (Orpak, Gasboy) and the 32-bit ARM build appear only in S9046. Verified: G1027 CyberAv3ngers does not mention IOControl, so the attribution is single-source. IRGC affiliation kept out of the reference."}
{"id": "mitre-h16", "question": "The 2017 attack on a petrochemical plant's safety instrumented system controllers stayed hidden for a while. What eventually exposed it?", "reference": "A safety trip caused by an issue in the malware.", "relevant": [{"path": "campaigns/C0030_triton-safety-instrumented-system-attack.md", "evidence": "The incident was eventually discovered due to a safety trip that occurred as a result of an issue in the malware"}], "answerable": true, "tags": ["hard", "campaign", "ics", "enterprise", "base", "real-incident"], "notes": "Indirect: must resolve the description to C0030 Triton Safety Instrumented System Attack (TEMP.Veles). Verified: 'safety trip' occurs only in C0030; the T0828 procedure line (also in T0828) about tripping a controller into a failed safe state describes the shutdown, not how the attack was discovered."}
{"id": "mitre-h17", "question": "Our threat feed describes an espionage group that has hit oil and gas, telecom, aviation and ISP organizations in the Middle East and Africa since 2017, and notes its TTPs look like APT33's and OilRig's. Does ATT&CK treat it as one of those two groups?", "reference": "No. ATT&CK tracks it separately, as HEXANE (G1001).", "relevant": [{"path": "groups/G1001_hexane.md", "evidence": "HEXANE's TTPs appear similar to APT33 and OilRig but due to differences in victims and tools it is tracked as a separate entity"}], "answerable": true, "tags": ["hard", "group", "enterprise", "ics", "base", "yes-no", "attribution"], "notes": "Must identify HEXANE (aka Lyceum, Siamesekitten, Spirlin) from the victim profile. Verified: neither G0064 APT33 nor G0049 OilRig mentions HEXANE, and the 'separate entity' reasoning occurs only in G1001. Context (not required in an answer): tracked separately because of differences in victims and tools."}
{"id": "mitre-h18", "question": "Our OT network monitor flagged a host sweeping a whole controller subnet with EtherNet/IP discovery traffic. ATT&CK's ICS detection guidance for this kind of scan cites a module of a known ICS attack toolkit as its documented example. Which module?", "reference": "The CODECALL module of PIPEDREAM/INCONTROLLER.", "relevant": [{"path": "techniques/T0846_remote-system-discovery.md", "evidence": "Documented in the PIPEDREAM/INCONTROLLER toolset's CODECALL module for EtherNet/IP device discovery"}], "answerable": true, "tags": ["hard", "detection", "ics", "base", "same-name-twin", "procedure"], "notes": "Buried in analytic AN2171 (Embedded) of DET0739 in ICS T0846 Remote System Discovery; the Enterprise twin T1018 has different analytics. Verified: 'CODECALL' occurs in no other file, including S1045 INCONTROLLER."}
{"id": "mitre-vh06", "question": "WolfRAT was built from leaked code of an older Android RAT. Which RAT was that, and what did it originally cost to buy?", "reference": "Dendroid (S0301); it sold for $300.", "relevant": [{"path": "software/S0489_wolfrat.md", "evidence": "WolfRAT is malware based on a leaked version of Dendroid that has primarily targeted Thai users"}, {"path": "software/S0301_dendroid.md", "evidence": "The RAT was available for purchase for $300 and came bundled with a utility to inject the RAT into legitimate applications"}], "answerable": true, "tags": ["very-hard", "software", "mobile", "base", "multi-hop", "number"], "notes": "Two hops: only the WolfRAT file says it is based on leaked Dendroid code, and only the Dendroid file gives the $300 price (Dendroid's file never mentions WolfRAT). Verified: '$300' and 'leaked version of Dendroid' each occur in exactly one file."}
{"id": "mitre-vh07", "question": "In the 2017 Dallas siren incident, on what radio frequency were the attackers thought to have captured the command broadcasts, and how many sirens did they then send commands to?", "reference": "700 MHz; 156 sirens.", "relevant": [{"path": "techniques/T0887_wireless-sniffing.md", "evidence": "adversaries likely captured wireless command message broadcasts on a 700 MHz frequency during a regular test of the system"}, {"path": "techniques/T0848_rogue-master.md", "evidence": "adversaries used a rogue master to send command messages to the 156 distributed sirens across the city"}], "answerable": true, "tags": ["very-hard", "technique", "ics", "base", "multi-hop", "number", "real-incident"], "notes": "Needs two files: the frequency is only in T0887 Wireless Sniffing, the siren count only in T0848 Rogue Master (description and its AN1924 analytic). Verified: T0813 and T1692.001 also mention the Dallas incident but state neither fact."}
{"id": "mitre-vh08", "question": "The Iranian group whose main persona claimed the March 2026 attack on Stryker Corporation has one campaign of its own in ATT&CK. Which campaign is it, and roughly how long were the intruders inside the victim's networks before it turned destructive?", "reference": "HomeLand Justice (C0038); about 14 months.", "relevant": [{"path": "groups/G1055_void-manticore.md", "evidence": "Handala Hack, its current primary persona, which has claimed activity against Israeli and U.S. entities, including a March 2026 attack against Stryker Corporation"}, {"path": "campaigns/C0038_homeland-justice.md", "evidence": "maintained persistence for approximately 14 months prior to the destructive phase of the operation"}], "answerable": true, "tags": ["very-hard", "campaign", "enterprise", "base", "multi-hop", "attribution", "temporal", "real-incident", "recent"], "notes": "Hops: Stryker -> VOID MANTICORE (G1055, whose Campaigns list holds only C0038) -> C0038 for the 14-month dwell time (May 2021 to the July 2022 destructive phase). C0038 never mentions Stryker or Handala Hack, and G1055 never gives the dwell time. T1119 also links VOID MANTICORE to 'the Stryker operation' in a procedure line, but only G1055 names Stryker Corporation and March 2026."}
{"id": "mitre-u11", "question": "Which iOS update did Apple release to patch the vulnerabilities exploited in Operation Triangulation?", "reference": "The documents don't say. The corpus describes Operation Triangulation's zero-click iMessage exploits and lists the CVEs it used, but not which iOS release fixed them.", "answerable": false, "tags": ["unanswerable", "campaign", "mobile", "base"], "notes": "Tempting because C0054 lists CVE-2023-41990/32435/32434/38606. Verified: no 'patch', 'iOS 15.x/16.5' or Apple fix mention in C0054, TriangleDB, Binary Validator or T1658/T1404; the only iOS version near this topic is Pegasus for iOS compromising iOS 16.6 (a distractor)."}
{"id": "mitre-u12", "question": "How many wrong passcode entries does ATT&CK's mobile Enterprise Policy mitigation say should trigger a full device wipe?", "reference": "The documents don't say. The corpus says enterprises can have a device wipe all data if an incorrect passcode is entered too many times, without giving a number.", "answerable": false, "tags": ["unanswerable", "mitigation", "mobile", "base", "number"], "notes": "M1012 and T1461 only say 'too many times' / 'several failed unlock attempts'. Verified by grepping wipe/failed/incorrect passcode across mobile files; the Enterprise M1036 (3-5 attempts) and M1018 (five failed logins) figures are about account lockout, not wiping a mobile device."}
{"id": "mitre-u13", "question": "How many people were arrested in the international law-enforcement operation that disrupted FluBot?", "reference": "The documents don't say. The corpus says an international law enforcement operation of 11 countries disrupted FluBot's spread, but gives no arrest figures.", "answerable": false, "tags": ["unanswerable", "software", "mobile", "base", "number"], "notes": "Tempting because S1067 mentions the 11-country operation. Verified: 'arrest' appears only in Cobalt Group and T1464 (cellular-jamming arrests), and no file names or quantifies the FluBot takedown beyond the country count."}
